WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.120
So I took a look at some questions from a Korean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.360
university entrance exam and I was&nbsp;
pretty shocked by how hard they were.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:14.800
This is the CSAT and I guess it's the&nbsp;
South Korean version of the American SAT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.200
which you may be more familiar&nbsp;
with. But I think this Korean exam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.200
ranks as much more difficult and is right up&nbsp;
there with some of the world's toughest exams.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.440
It is taken by Korean high school students and&nbsp;
the score determines which university they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:38.240
go to. It is very competitive because many in&nbsp;
Korea believe that a university's ranking makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.800
a huge difference to one's career prospects. To&nbsp;
get into those top unis you might only be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:51.200
get one or two questions wrong on the whole exam&nbsp;
but that becomes seemingly impossible when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.840
realize that the exam covers five different topics&nbsp;
on one day and takes over eight hours to complete.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.320
I think one of the most difficult sections&nbsp;
is mathematics. It covers calculus;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:10.160
with limits, derivatives, integration,&nbsp;
trig calculus, even integration by parts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:17.760
Plenty of conic sections, vectors, dot products,&nbsp;
3D geometry, probability and statistics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.960
And students would get three to&nbsp;
four minutes to solve each question.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:29.200
For question 15 we are told that&nbsp;
two functions: y equals a to the x,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:37.360
and y equals root three, meet at the point A.&nbsp;
There's also this point B which is at four zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:44.480
and we're asked what is the product of all the&nbsp;
values of the little a such that the straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:53.120
lines AB and OA are perpendicular to each other.&nbsp;
Now if you ask me that sounds pretty tricky.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:00.320
I heard the notorious questions hide near the end&nbsp;
so let's check out question 30 on this 2020 paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:07.440
What it says is that we have these two functions:&nbsp;
one is a log and one is an exponential, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:16.000
meet at only one point when little a can be said&nbsp;
to be f(t). It's a function of this variable t.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:23.120
Then we're asked to find the square of&nbsp;
f prime when t is equal to one-third.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.440
Have a think about what you might want&nbsp;
to do here, i'll come back at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.160
and show my version of a solution but&nbsp;
first let's look at some other sections.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:40.160
There's also Korean, English, History, Science&nbsp;
and some other optional papers. The english test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:47.200
starts off with a listening section and for this&nbsp;
the whole country goes silent, no planes take off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:54.560
or land in South Korea for that 30 minute period,&nbsp;
so that shows how seriously this exam is taken.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:01.920
For this recent question they had to listen to&nbsp;
what seems like a youtube intro and identify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:08.160
purpose of the paragraph but later in the english&nbsp;
section the questions get a lot more abstract with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:14.080
language that even a native english speaker would&nbsp;
find intense. There's a question about an African&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:20.240
proverb "till the lions have their historians,&nbsp;
tales of hunting will always glorify the hunter".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.720
And there's this paragraph here asking us&nbsp;
about the fact that "people don't usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:32.640
think of touch as a temporal phenomenon but it&nbsp;
is every bit as time-based as it is spatial".

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:40.640
The physics paper contains all the usual suspects&nbsp;
but seems mostly based on conceptual understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:45.200
rather than heavy calculations.&nbsp;
Understanding an eclipse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.240
how to determine the wavelength&nbsp;
of a laser, and orbital mechanics.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:04:01.520
Here is just a glance at chemistry,&nbsp;
biology, and geology and astrophysics.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:11.760
500 000 students take the exam each year but&nbsp;
around 20% of those are people taking it again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:17.200
because they weren't happy with their previous&nbsp;
score. Those students may keep studying for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:23.280
same exam year after year until they can get&nbsp;
into a good university and move on with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:30.000
life. South Korea is an extremely well-educated&nbsp;
country, their performance as well as enrollment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
and graduation rates are ranked among the top few&nbsp;
in the world. They have around 80 percent of high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:43.120
school graduates going on to university so it may&nbsp;
come as no surprise that there is also a booming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:50.640
private coaching industry centered around passing&nbsp;
this exam. They call them hagwons or cram schools.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:57.120
So let's take a look back at that math problem. It&nbsp;
seems you first have to be smart enough to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.440
out that if our two functions would look like this&nbsp;
for the exponential and this for the log that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.960
they touch at only one point it would be something&nbsp;
like this, where the functions are tangent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:16.160
do not intersect. So we could set our function 1&nbsp;
equal to function 2 to find that point where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:22.880
touch. That will give us this star equation here&nbsp;
but that doesn't immediately take us any further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:30.240
instead we need a bit of a brain wave that we can&nbsp;
also set the derivatives, that is dy/dx, equal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.560
each other because at this point, since they're&nbsp;
tangent, the slopes of each line would be equal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:42.560
So we can differentiate each side and set them&nbsp;
equal. Noticing that our right hand side hasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.680
changed during this process we can replace&nbsp;
it with the definition we had for it earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:54.720
giving us a version without any a and just t's&nbsp;
and x's, which can simplify down to give us that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.720
all of this is equal to one and i'll call that&nbsp;
square because we're also going to use that later.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:06.640
This might seem like another dead end but we have&nbsp;
to remember that we're being asked to find f prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:16.480
of t which I prefer to think of as da/dt and how&nbsp;
would we get a da/dt? Well we could go back up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:22.320
the equation that we started with and if we were&nbsp;
to differentiate both sides of this with respect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:29.840
to t, this little a in here due to implicit&nbsp;
differentiation would give us a da/dt term.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:36.000
So that's how we can get it. I differentiate both&nbsp;
sides of it with respect to t, remembering that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:42.640
a is a function of t, a bit of product rule a&nbsp;
bit of chain rule and you'll end up with this.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:48.640
Now we're getting good at replacing this term in&nbsp;
here which crops up again. I'm going to replace it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:54.400
with the result of that initial differentiation&nbsp;
so i'll end up with this line here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:01.920
Now really we want to get da/dt by itself since&nbsp;
that's what we're interested in. We can get rid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:09.360
some of these terms by multiplying both sides by&nbsp;
x minus t over t cubed. And also remembering that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:16.400
earlier we found that all this was merely equal&nbsp;
to one because that crops up right here we'll set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:22.400
that equal to one, that simplifies things down&nbsp;
quite a bit. A little bit more rearranging and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:29.600
we get something for da/dt which is the same as&nbsp;
what they're calling f dash of t and because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:36.400
wanted f dash of t at t is equal to one third,&nbsp;
we can put in one third for our value of t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:44.000
giving minus eight. And then they want all&nbsp;
of that squared so that would be sixty-four.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:49.680
Perhaps the calculus and algebra involved&nbsp;
here is not that advanced but the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.680
it's arranged and the fact you need to&nbsp;
keep going back to results you found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:59.360
earlier and somehow knowing how to get your way&nbsp;
out of these seemingly dead ends does make this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:05.120
problem really difficult. Certainly a stretch to&nbsp;
be able to do this in just three or four minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:10.480
and remember for this exam just thinking&nbsp;
that you can skip questions that are too hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.960
is not really a feasible option if&nbsp;
you want to get those top scores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:20.000
because getting just one or two wrong on the&nbsp;
whole thing can actually make the difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.480
If you want to rank at the top I think&nbsp;
you're expected to be able to do all of them.

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:30.000
Best of luck to all students studying for this&nbsp;
exam or a similar one. Thanks for watching this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.800
video and you can check out others in the playlist&nbsp;
to see how other countries might do things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

